        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ad8f4e03-14f6-5fbe-8b49-4ec7addaa04d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Carpentaria acuminata, Carpentaria, Panax, ginseng\n- Plant taxonomy: Araliaceae\n- Places: Northern Territory, Australia, Gulf of Carpentaria\n- Natural systems: Port McArthur Tidal Wetlands System\n\nFacts:\n- Carpentaria acuminata, also called the carpentaria palm, is the only species in the genus Carpentaria.\n- Carpentaria acuminata is native to tropical coastal regions in the north of the Northern Territory, Australia.\n- The genus Panax, also called ginseng, belongs to the family Araliaceae.\n- Ginseng refers to any of several slow-growing perennial plants with fleshy roots in the genus Panax of the family Araliaceae.\n- The Port McArthur Tidal Wetlands System comprises a 994 km tract of tidal wetlands on the south-west coast of the Gulf of Carpentaria in the Northern Territory of Australia.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4e921716-f801-5171-bba7-2ef8168bf270",
